---

Workspace Philosophy

Each package has one clear responsibility.

Packages communicate through public APIs only. Internal implementation details remain private.


---

Apps

dashboard

The web interface for viewing project reports, architecture, plugins, and AI insights.


---

api

Hosts the Ohira REST API, exposing endpoints such as:

GET /status
GET /context
GET /report
POST /analyze


---

docs

Documentation website built with a static site generator (for example, Next.js with MDX).


---

website

Marketing site containing product information, roadmap, and download links.


---

Packages

core

The orchestration engine.

Responsibilities:

Load configuration

Discover plugins

Run analysis pipeline

Build the knowledge graph

Handle caching and errors


No framework-specific logic belongs here.


---

cli

Implements commands such as:

ohira init
ohira analyze
ohira report
ohira doctor
ohira plugins


---

sdk

Defines the public interfaces used by plugin authors.

Includes:

Plugin interfaces

Collector interfaces

Event system

Utility helpers



---

knowledge

Defines:

Entity models

Relationships

Graph builders

Query utilities


This package is the heart of Ohira's intelligence model.


---

reporter

Responsible for generating:

Markdown

HTML

JSON


Future:

PDF

OpenAPI

Architecture diagrams



---

context

Produces optimized project context for AI systems.

Supports different output profiles for:

ChatGPT

Claude

Gemini

Cursor

GitHub Copilot



---

shared

Contains reusable:

Utilities

Constants

Types

Logging

Configuration

Error classes


No business logic should live here.


---

Plugin Packages

Each plugin follows the same structure.

Example:

packages/plugins/prisma/

src/
├── collector.ts
├── analyzer.ts
├── mapper.ts
├── types.ts
├── index.ts
└── plugin.ts


---

Package Dependency Rules

Allowed:

CLI
    ↓
Core
    ↓
SDK
    ↓
Shared

Plugins
    ↓
SDK

Reporter
    ↓
Knowledge

Not allowed:

Plugins importing other plugins.

Core importing plugins directly.

Shared depending on application packages.

Circular dependencies.



---

Build System

Recommended tools:

pnpm for package management

Turborepo for build orchestration

TypeScript Project References for fast incremental builds

ESLint for linting

Prettier for formatting

Vitest for testing



---

Naming Conventions

Packages:

@ohira/core
@ohira/cli
@ohira/sdk
@ohira/context

Plugins:

@ohira/plugin-prisma
@ohira/plugin-next
@ohira/plugin-git
@ohira/plugin-docker


---

Branching Strategy

Main branches:

main
develop

Feature branches:

feature/plugin-prisma
feature/rest-api
feature/dashboard

Bug fixes:

fix/context-engine

Documentation:

docs/prd-update


---

Release Strategy

Semantic Versioning:

0.1.0
0.2.0
0.5.0
1.0.0

Patch: bug fixes

Minor: new features

Major: breaking changes



---

Testing Strategy

Every package should include:

Unit tests

Integration tests (where applicable)


Core and plugins should also have compatibility tests to ensure they work together.


---

Coding Standards

TypeScript in strict mode.

Public APIs documented with TSDoc.

Functions should be small and focused.

Prefer composition over inheritance.

Avoid global mutable state.

Keep packages framework-agnostic where possible.



---

Continuous Integration

Each pull request should run:

1. Install dependencies


2. Type check


3. Lint


4. Unit tests


5. Integration tests


6. Build affected packages



No code should be merged if any required check fails.


---

Contributor Experience

A new contributor should be able to:

pnpm install
pnpm build
pnpm test
pnpm dev

and have a working development environment in a few minutes.


---

Future Growth

This monorepo structure is designed to support future additions such as:

VS Code extension

JetBrains plugin

MCP server

GitHub App

Desktop application

Mobile companion app

Cloud synchronization service

Enterprise edition


without restructuring the repository.


---

Guiding Principle

> "Small packages. Clear responsibilities. Stable interfaces."



By keeping packages focused and loosely coupled, Ohira can grow from a single CLI tool into a robust developer platform while remaining maintainable.
